oss":"saying"}],"gloss":"saying"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"உள்ளதும்","parts":[{"text":"உள்ளது","lemma":"uḷḷatu","gloss":"what is present"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"um","gloss":"and"}],"gloss":"what is present"},{"surface":"இல்லதும்","parts":[{"text":"இல்லது","lemma":"illatu","gloss":"what is absent"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"um","gloss":"and"}],"gloss":"what is absent"},{"surface":"அறியாது","parts":[{"text":"அறியாது","lemma":"aṟi","gloss":"not knowing"}],"gloss":"not knowing"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"ஆங்கு","parts":[{"text":"ஆங்கு","lemma":"āṅku","gloss":"thus"}],"gloss":"thus"},{"surface":"அமைந்தன்றால்","parts":[{"text":"அமைந்தன்றால்","lemma":"அமைந்தன்றால்","gloss":"it is established"}],"gloss":"it is established","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"வாழ்க","parts":[{"text":"வாழ்க","lemma":"vāḻ","gloss":"may live long"}],"gloss":"may live long"},{"surface":"அவன்","parts":[{"text":"அவன்","lemma":"avaṉ","gloss":"his"}],"gloss":"his"},{"surface":"தாளே","parts":[{"text":"தாள்","lemma":"tāḷ","gloss":"feet"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ē","gloss":"indeed"}],"gloss":"feet"}]}],"literal":"Soft land's field ploughmen / hard land's bullock having let go / short hare's tender roasted meat with / long scabbard-fish's many steamed dishes / old rice abundantly having eaten / shrub wild-jasmine's flower having worn / dense drum with birds having chased away / boiled paddy's toddy consuming / house fowl's green crop now / forest fowl's loud voice with / water fowl's cry making it move / bamboo like soft shoulders with / peacock like gentle grace they / parrots chasing away now / wide muddy field's birds making them fly / there many good fields adorning / fame filled great glory's / small-eyed elephant's rare to obtain Tittan's / never-ending good fame's Uranthai's east / long-handed Vel chieftain's highly guarded Pidavur / virtuous-named Sathan's kinsmen we are, O Lord! / Previous day's midday desert path having suffered and grieved / sun fully gone dense darkness evening / his gate having appeared my relationship because of declaring / sweet sound kin-sounding sharp sound Tadari drum with / there standing me having seen / even a little he did not delay, much he did not speak / rare ornaments to come he graced, desiring / wonderfully saying besides speaking, having given, his house / gold like wife having shown, 'him / like me protect' he said, because of that / him I will not forget, others I will not think of / wide world greatly even if it burns / much in the sky comet even if it appears / pond star with stem even if it smokes / large field paddy's crane's claw like cooked rice / fresh meat curry's roasted meat with to eat / harvest alone or flood may take saying / what is present and what is absent not knowing / thus it is established, may live long his feet!","translation":"The ploughmen of the soft wetlands, having unyoked their bullocks from the hard drylands, feast abundantly on old rice served with the tender roasted meat of short hares and various steamed dishes of long scabbard-fish. Wearing wild jasmine flowers from the shrubs, they chase away birds with dense drumbeats and drink toddy brewed from boiled paddy. Meanwhile, the house fowls in the green crops, along with the loud calls of forest fowls and the cries of water fowls, make the birds fly away. Women with bamboo-like soft shoulders and peacock-like grace chase away parrots from the fields. In that region of Pidavur, lying to the east of Uranthai—the city of never-ending fame belonging to King Tittan who possesses small-eyed elephants—rules the long-handed Vel chieftain. We are the kinsmen of Sathan, who bears a virtuous name, O Lord! On a previous day, after wandering and suffering through the desert path at midday, I arrived at his gate in the dense darkness of evening when the sun had fully set. As I declared my kinship, standing there with my sweet-toned, sharp-sounding Tadari drum, he saw me. Without delaying even a moment or speaking too many words, he desired to grace me with rare ornaments. Speaking wondrously, he gave them to me, and showing his gold-like wife, he told her, 'Cherish him just like me.' Because of that, I shall never forget him, nor shall I think of any other patron! Even if the wide world burns with intense heat, even if a comet appears in the sky, and even if the stars smoke, his fields are so fertile that we feast on cooked rice resembling crane's claws, along with fresh meat curry. Not caring whether the harvest is rich or a flood sweeps it away, and without worrying about what is present or absent, we live in abundance. Thus is his nature established. May his feet live long!","modern":"மென்பuலத்து வயல் உழவர்கள், வன்புலத்து எருதுகளை அவிழ்த்துவிட்டு, முயலின் மென்மையான இறைச்சியுடனும், வாளை மீனின் அவியலுடனும், பழைய சோற்றை மிகுதியாக உண்கின்றனர். முல்லைப் பூக்களைச் சூடி, பறையடித்துப் பறவைகளை ஓட்டி, நெல்லின் கள்ளைக் குடிக்கின்றனர். வீட்டுக் கோழிகள், காட்டுக்கோழிகள், நீர்க்கோழிகளின் ஒலிகளால் பறவைகள் விரைகின்றன. மூங்கில் போன்ற தோள்களும் மயில் போன்ற சாயலும் உடைய பெண்கள் கிளிகளை ஓட்டுகின்றனர். இத்தகைய வளமான வயல்கள் சூழ்ந்ததும், புகழ்பெற்ற தித்தனின் உறையூருக்குக் கிழக்கே உள்ளதுமான பிடவூரின் தலைவன் அறநெறி மிக்க சாத்தன் ஆவான். பெருமானே, நாங்கள் அவனுடைய சுற்றத்தினர் ஆவோம்! முன்பொரு நாள், நண்பகலில் பாலை நிலத்தில் நடந்து வருந்தி, கதிரவன் மறைந்த மாலைப் பொழுதில் அவனது வாயிலில் தோன்றி, என் சுற்றத்தை இசைத்து, என் தடாரிப் பறையை முழக்கினேன். அங்கே நின்ற என்னைக் கண்டு, அவன் சிறிதும் தாமதிக்காமல், அதிகம் பேசாமல், எனக்குப் பரிசில்களை வழங்கினான். தன் பொன் போன்ற மனைவியைக் காட்டி, 'இவனை என்னைப் போலப் பாதுகாப்பாயாக' என்று கூறினான். அதனால், நான் அவனை ஒருபோதும் மறக்க மாட்டேன்; வேறு யாரையும் நினைக்க மாட்டேன். உலகம் வெப்பத்தால் வருந்தினாலும், வானில் எரிமீன் தோன்றினாலும், கோள்கள் நிலைமாறிப் புகைந்தாலும், அவனது பெரிய வயல்களில் விளையும் நெல்லின் சோற்றை, புதிய இறைச்சிக் கறியுடன் நாங்கள் உண்கிறோம். விளைச்சல் பெருகினாலும், வெள்ளம் கொண்டாலும், உள்ளதையும் இல்லதையும் கவலைப்படாமல் வாழ்கிறோம். அவனது கொடைத்தன்மை அவ்வாறு நிலைபெற்றுள்ளது. அவனது திருவடிகள் வாழ்க!","explanation":"The poet Madurai Nakkīrar sings in praise of Peruñcāttan, the son of Piṭavūr Kiḻār, in the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai (theme of praise). The poet describes himself as a poor bard who, after suffering through a harsh desert path, arrived at Sathan's mansion at nightfall. Sathan immediately welcomed him, showered him with rare gifts, and instructed his wife to treat the bard as his own equal. The poem beautifully contrasts the harshness of the bard's journey with the lush, fertile landscape of Pidavur, where agriculture thrives regardless of cosmic portents or droughts. Even if natural disasters strike or stars smoke, Sathan's generosity ensures that his dependents never face want. Key words like 'அறப்பெயர்ச் சாத்தன்' (virtuous-named Sathan) and 'என்போல் போற்று' (protect him like me) highlight the patron's exceptional nobility and hospitality.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}